186:
The period of staying in the interval from the start of the effort to withdraw the organ that has been so inserted is called 'ṭhita' (staying). In the Samantapāsādikā commentary, however, referring to the period of staying after ceasing all effort upon reaching the emission of the woman's impurity, it is said 'at the time of emission'. 'Uddharaṇa' (withdrawal) means the period of removing it until the tip. 'Sādiyati' (consents) means establishing the mind of indulgence (sevanacitta). 'Asādhāraṇa-vinicchaya' (uncommon decision) means a decision that is not common to all training rules such as taking what is not given (adinnādāna). 

'Sādhāraṇa-vinicchayattha' means for the purpose of a common decision by way of the Parivāra. 'Mātikā' (matrix) means mother, in the sense of one who generates. 'Nidāna' (origin) is so called because it makes known what was unknown by way of the location of the teaching. 'Paññatti' (regulation) is so called because it is regulated; its variety is 'paññatti-vidhi' (method of regulation). 'Aṅga' (factor) is so called because it makes known (the cause). 'Samuṭṭhāna' (origination) is so called because offenses arise through this cause; its variety is 'samuṭṭhāna-vidhi' (method of origination). In the term 'vajjakamma-ppabhedañca', the word 'pabheda' (variety) should be applied to each: 'vajja-ppabheda' (variety of faults) and 'kamma-ppabheda' (variety of actions). 'Tattha tattha' means in those various training rules. 

'Paññattiṭṭhāna' means the place where the regulation was established, meaning the location where the training rules were regulated. In this context, 'puggala' (person) refers only to the first offender (ādikammika); thus it is said: 'A person is one with reference to whom a particular training rule is regulated,' meaning those various individuals. In this regard, there are these verses: 

'Sudinna, Dhaniya, the many monks (of the Suttas), and those living on the banks of the Vaggumudā river; 
Seyyasaka, Udāyi, the monks of Āḷavī, Channa, and Mettiya-Bhummajaka; 
Devadatta, Assaji and Punabbasu, the Chabbaggiya monks, Upananda and another monk; 
Hatthaka, the Elder Anuruddha, the Sattarasavaggiya monks, and the Elder Cūḷapanthaka; 
Belaṭṭhasīsa, the Elder Ānanda, Sāgata, the one named Ariṭṭha, and the Elder Nanda—these twenty-three monks are the first offenders. 

Sundarīnandā, Thullanandā, a certain nun of the Chabbaggiya group; 
Caṇḍakāḷī, the many nuns, and two other nuns; 
Among the nuns, only these seven are the first offenders.' 

'Tassa tassa puggalassa' means with reference to whichever person, such as Sudinna, the training rule was regulated. 'Paññatti' means the primary regulation. A regulation established after the primary regulation is called 'anupaññatti' (supplementary regulation). A regulation established before a fault has appeared is called 'anuppannapaññatti'. A regulation established for all regions, such as the Middle Country and the border regions, is called 'sabbatthapaññatti' (universal regulation).
